><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20250702111210+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="71561cdc-76b7-4e3f-98e3-e6d59e2d5cfc" date="D:20250702111219+10'00'" name="3613a357-abe7-4034-a246-1a524deae585" icon="Comment" page="11" rect="165.703323,358.509399,189.703323,382.509399" subject="Sticky Note" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c:</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:bold;font-style:normal"
> </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>This study identified the following themes necessary to achieve athlete performance&#xD;health from the perspective of stakeholders in Australia’s HPSS: ‘Leadership’, ‘Skilled&#xD;people’, ‘Performance health culture’, ‘Operational effectiveness’, and ‘Integrated strategy’.&#xD;To address these themes, we identified items that sporting organizations can incorporate&#xD;in their sport strategies and operational plans to maximize the health and performance of&#xD;their athletes.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="11" rect="595.276001,290.509399,779.276001,382.509399"
/></text
